This little document is for how to implement each command, basic pseudocode, and helpful pwsh cmdlets for the job.

hug <list>
This creates a new list. It makes sense for hug to rely on a single JSON file, so the <list> will be an object with fields of 
<items>. By default, the item will contain nothing!

JSON file that hug is looking for is hug.json. By default, this file will contain nothing! 
hug.json is going to be an extremely basic json. Structured as such:
list: {
    item: value,
    item: value,
    item: value,
}

Real example could be
"urls": {
    "github": "https://github.com",
    "gitlab": "https://gitlab.com",
    "vimcs": "https://vim.rtorr.com"
}

This same command also lists everything in the list if it already exists, so the Json needs to be read to see if the list already
exists.

pwsh: Get-Content "file.json" | ConvertFrom-Json


hug <list> <item> <value>
This creates a new item <item> with the value <value> in the target <list>. Expects the list to already be created.

Check if list exists > create new field in list that has the key of item and the value of value

Example: $file = Get-Content "file.json" | ConvertFrom-Json
$file | rg "(?i)list" > check if returns true or false
Figure out how to add to an existing JSON object, if we use our urls example above...
"urls": {
    "github": "https://github.com",
    "gitlab": "https://gitlab.com",
    "vimcheats": "https://vim.rtorr.com"
}

